\title[Hamiltonian cycles in Cayley graphs of squarefree order]
{Hamiltonian Cycles in Cayley Graphs of Squarefree Order:\\
Voltages, Twisted Direction Sequences,\\ and the Complete Order-$pq$ Theorem}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{16 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop, from first principles, a toolkit for constructing hamiltonian cycles in Cayley
graphs of finite groups of squarefree order, and use it to give a complete, hypothesis-free
treatment of the two-prime case together with substantial partial results in the four-prime
case that motivates the programme. The central results are: a quotient-free \emph{factor group
lemma} phrased in terms of the voltage of a periodic word; a \emph{direction-sequence
criterion} for two commuting generators, with closing condition $m \mid \sum_{j<k} d_j$ and a
parity-free resolution showing that every two-generator abelian Cayley graph of this shape is
hamiltonian; the \emph{twisted} analogue for metacyclic groups $\langle a\rangle \rtimes
\langle b\rangle$ with $bab^{-1} = a^{e}$, whose closing condition is the geometric congruence
$m \mid \sum_{j<k} d_j e^{j}$ and is automatic when $e-1$ is invertible modulo $m$; and a
\emph{coset-pair criterion} settling the residual partial-transversal configuration by an
explicit word whose voltage $B\,(xBx^{-1})$ never vanishes. Combining these with a reduction
theorem we prove that \emph{every connected Cayley graph of a group of order $pq$ is
hamiltonian}, for all distinct primes $p \ne q$, with no restriction on the connection set and
no commutativity assumption. For order $pqrs$ with four distinct primes we settle the cyclic,
abelian, dihedral, and coprimely-twisted metacyclic families, exhibit explicit witnesses, and
record sharpness statements.
\end{abstract}

